  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY...\n\nEarly this morning, the much talked about mid/upper ridge remains \ncentered over New Mexico while a subtle shortwave trough slides \nsoutheastward around the northwestern periphery of this ridge across \nthe central plains. Meanwhile, the LLJ is nosing into a modest mid-\nlevel baroclinic zone draped across northern Kansas and southern \nNebraska. As a result, mid-level WAA is overspreading this \narea, and the resultant ascent is supporting the development of \nshowers and storms early this morning across portions of \nnorthern and northeastern Kansas. A few showers may develop \nacross central Kansas, but currently, coverage will likely \nremain quite isolated this morning. \n\nThroughout the day, the aforementioned weak shortwave trough will \ncontinue to linger over Kansas as it lumbers south and east across \nthe region. Additionally, the primary axis of deep and rich moisture \nwill be placed over eastern Kansas with PWATs well over 2 inches \nacross areas along and east of I-135. With modest upper level \nsupport, along with deep and rich moisture creating an uncapped \nenvironment, isolated thunderstorms are possible generally along and \neast of Salina-Wichita line this afternoon and evening. Given very \nlittle shear (around 15 knots of 0-6 km shear), the expectation is \nfor this activity to be quite disorganized, and severe storms \nappear unlikely this afternoon and evening. That being said, wet\nmicrobursts could produce 40-50 mph wind gusts in very \nlocalized areas. Another consequence of this rich moisture \nreturning to the region will be the high likelihood of locations\nacross southern and southeastern Kansas experiencing another \nafternoon of heat indices exceeding 105. \n\nWith the loss of diurnal heating this evening, chances of showers \nand storms will briefly decrease. However, additional shower and \nthunderstorm development is anticipated overnight as widespread mid-\nlevel WAA overspreads much of the area. Given the elevated nature of \nthis activity, and that instability will be fairly limited (under \n1000 J/kg of MUCAPE), severe storms appear unlikely. The main \nconcerns for showers and storms overnight tonight into Thursday \nmorning will be brief heavy rainfall, occasional lightning strikes, \nand maybe some locally gusty winds. This activity will linger \nthrough much of Thursday morning, and may even last well into the \nafternoon hours across far eastern Kansas. Meanwhile, a brief warm-\nup will be felt across central and south-central Kansas with \nincreasing chances of another day with afternoon highs exceeding 100 \ndegrees. It's possible that heat indices could exceed 105 again \nduring the afternoon and evening hours, especially across south-\ncentral Kansas.\n \nMid-range guidance continues to suggest a stronger shortwave trough \nwill dig into the northern and central plains Thursday evening \ninto Friday morning. As a result, more widespread showers and \nstorms are forecast Thursday night into Friday morning across \nthe entire forecast area. Despite warm mid-level temperatures \ncontributing to widespread convective inhibition, forcing for \nascent from the incoming upper trough along with a strengthening\nLLJ should support the maintenance of thunderstorms during the \novernight hours as they sweep across Nebraska and most of \nKansas. There is still some uncertainty in the coverage of \nstorms the further south you go, and it's possible portions of \nfar southern Kansas miss out on most, if not all, of the \nrainfall. That being said, those who do see a storm late \nThursday night into Friday morning could see marginally severe \nwind gusts up to 60 mph as well as some brief heavy rainfall. \nSome of this activity could linger throughout the day on Friday \nacross far eastern Kansas, but coverage will be fairly isolated.\nIn the wake of the shower and thunderstorm activity, a more \nrobust cold front will sweep across the region on Friday; \nhowever, afternoon highs will still manage to climb into the mid\nto upper 90s. The cooler air finally arrives Friday night with \nlows expected to drop into the 60s.\n\n     SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY...\n\nMid to long range deterministic and ensemble model guidance suggests \nthe upper level pattern will be momentarily locked in for a\ncouple of days this weekend with troughing across the eastern \nCONUS, and a strong ridge parked over the Desert Southwest. This\nwill put Kansas awkwardly in the middle of these features with \nnortherly upper flow remaining quite persistent through the \nweekend. At the surface, a secondary surge of relatively cooler \nair is forecast to arrive on Saturday, and this should keep \ntemperatures around 5 to 10 degrees below average this weekend. \nGoing into early next week, mid to long range guidance continues\nto show a more ill-defined pattern across the central CONUS. \nHowever, it does appear that slight increases in heights aloft \nshould support a gradual warming of temperatures through the \nb",
